"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,175\nThe Learning Brain. Series 7: Digital Learning\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,435\nEpisode 12\n\n3\n00:00:07,515 --> 00:00:09,725\nDesigning for screens\n\n4\n00:00:12,525 --> 00:00:15,256\nAcross this series, we have looked at slides,\n\n5\n00:00:15,276 --> 00:00:18,319\nvideo, notes, devices, reading and course formats.\n\n6\n00:00:18,699 --> 00:00:24,064\nPut together, a clear picture emerges: screens are neither the problem nor the answer.\n\n7\n00:00:24,525 --> 00:00:25,621\nDesign is.\n\n8\n00:00:25,791 --> 00:00:27,463\nBefore a course or unit.\n\n9\n00:00:27,917 --> 00:00:29,729\nPlan short video segments,\n\n10\n00:00:29,737 --> 00:00:31,344\neach around one idea.\n\n11\n00:00:31,748 --> 00:00:34,610\nBuild slides with a key visual and few words,\n\n12\n00:00:34,630 --> 00:00:36,876\nand cut extras that don't explain.\n\n13\n00:00:37,290 --> 00:00:39,808\nOffer key readings in a printable form.\n\n14\n00:00:39,978 --> 00:00:40,650\nDuring.\n\n15\n00:00:40,888 --> 00:00:43,469\nPut questions inside videos and lectures,\n\n16\n00:00:43,598 --> 00:00:45,593\nto keep attention and build memory.\n\n17\n00:00:46,081 --> 00:00:49,465\nSet device norms, and use devices on purpose.\n\n18\n00:00:49,806 --> 00:00:52,470\nTeach note taking, not just note medium.\n\n19\n00:00:52,947 --> 00:00:56,617\nUse in person time for what only people in a room can do.\n\n20\n00:00:56,787 --> 00:00:57,437\nAfter.\n\n21\n00:00:57,536 --> 00:01:00,866\nGuide how students use recordings and speed controls: to review,\n\n22\n00:01:01,175 --> 00:01:05,479\nspaced over time, with saved time spent on practice.\n\n23\n00:01:05,865 --> 00:01:08,734\nCheck outcomes, not just clicks and views.\n\n24\n00:01:08,904 --> 00:01:09,886\nSome caution.\n\n25\n00:01:10,300 --> 00:01:13,471\nMany of these findings come from short lab studies,\n\n26\n00:01:13,496 --> 00:01:15,387\nand some, like note taking,\n\n27\n00:01:15,407 --> 00:01:16,662\nare still debated.\n\n28\n00:01:17,121 --> 00:01:19,519\nTreat them as strong starting points,\n\n29\n00:01:19,666 --> 00:01:22,217\nthen watch what happens in your own course.\n\n30\n00:01:22,387 --> 00:01:27,021\nOne thread runs through all of it: attention and working memory are limited.\n\n31\n00:01:27,530 --> 00:01:29,529\nGood digital design protects them.\n\n32\n00:01:29,875 --> 00:01:31,605\nIt removes what distracts,\n\n33\n00:01:31,766 --> 00:01:35,814\nsegments what overloads, and asks students to retrieve and think.\n\n34\n00:01:35,984 --> 00:01:36,776\nFor teaching.\n\n35\n00:01:37,171 --> 00:01:38,787\nPick one change for before,\n\n36\n00:01:38,807 --> 00:01:42,036\nduring and after your next online or recorded unit.\n\n37\n00:01:42,448 --> 00:01:45,355\nThen look at what students actually did and learned.\n\n38\n00:01:45,773 --> 00:01:47,683\nOne tip to share with students: one task,\n\n39\n00:01:47,930 --> 00:01:51,549\none screen, one goal per study session.\n\n40\n00:01:51,719 --> 00:01:53,547\nThe screen is just the window.\n\n41\n00:01:53,794 --> 00:01:55,891\nWhat students do there is the lesson.\n\n42\n00:01:56,220 --> 00:01:58,244\nThanks for watching series seven.\n\n"